.TH cpdir "" "Command"
.SH NAME
cpdir \- copy directory hierarchy
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBcpdir [\fIoption ...\fB] \fIdir1 dir2\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
cpdir -- copy directory hierarchy
Usage:	cpdir [ option ... ] dir1 dir2
Options:
	-d	Preserve last-modified date
	-e	Recover from errors and continue
	-r[n]	Recur n levels only (default: 1)
	-sname	Suppress copy of name, name is relative to dir1
	-t	Test and report errors - do not change anything
	-u	Update regular files if more recent
	-v	Verbose file by file account
.HE
.B cpdir
copies the directory hierarchy rooted at
.I dir1
onto
.IR dir2 ,
creating
.I dir2
(via
.BR mkdir )
if it does not already exist.
Regular files, subdirectories, and (for the superuser) device
special files and named pipes are all copied; hierarchies may
straddle device boundaries.
.PP
Files within
.I dir1
that have more than one link to each other are recreated as links
to each other under
.I dir2
instead of as separate copies.
Links to files outside the
.I dir1
hierarchy, or that cannot be recreated (for example, because the
target crosses a device boundary), are reported at the end of the
run as missed or broken external links, provided
.B \-v
or
.B \-t
was given.
.PP
If a target file corresponding to a source file already exists and
is not a directory,
.B cpdir
unlinks it before copying.
If
.I dir1
and
.I dir2
turn out to be the same directory (or one nested inside the copy of
the other), that directory is skipped to avoid an infinite copy.
.PP
Copied files get the source file's permission bits.
When run by the superuser, the owner, group, and full mode
(including the sticky bit) are preserved as well; otherwise the
invoking user owns the new files and the sticky bit is not copied.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Give each copied file the source file's last-modified time,
instead of the time at which it was copied.
.IP \fB\-e\fR
After an error, print a message and continue with the rest of the
hierarchy instead of exiting immediately.
.IP \fB\-r\fR[\fIn\fR]
Recur at most
.I n
levels into the source hierarchy; the contents of
.I dir1
itself are level 1.
A bare
.B \-r
with no
.I n
limits the copy to level 1 (no recursion into subdirectories).
Without
.BR \-r ,
.B cpdir
recurs without limit.
.IP \fB\-s\fIname\fR
Do not copy
.IR name ,
a pathname relative to
.IR dir1 .
May be repeated to suppress more than one name.
.IP \fB\-t\fR
Test only: report what would be done (implies
.B \-e
and verbose reporting) without creating, copying, linking, or
unlinking anything.
.IP \fB\-u\fR
Copy a regular file only if the source is newer than an existing
target, or if the target does not yet exist.
.IP \fB\-v\fR
Print a line for each file as it is copied, in addition to the
information printed under
.BR \-t .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cp ,
.BR mkdir ,
.B ln
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B cpdir
prints a usage message and exits 1 if given a bad option or not
exactly two directory arguments.
During the copy, it reports (and, without
.BR \-e ,
stops on) conditions such as a source or target that cannot be
found, opened, or read; a target that is not a directory or lacks
needed permission; a device boundary crossing; a link that could
not be recreated; or running out of memory.
With
.BR \-e ,
.B cpdir
exits 1 at the end if any error occurred.
